{"name":"Water Droplet","hex":"#e1e5dc","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"water","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An inorganic compound (of molecular formula .mw-parser-output .template-chem2-su{display:inline-block;font-size:80%;line-height:1;vertical-align:-0.35em}.mw-parser-output .template-chem2-su>span{display:block;text-align:left}.mw-parser-output sub.template-chem2-sub{font-size:80%;vertical-align:-0.35em}.mw-parser-output sup.template-chem2-sup{font-size:80%;vertical-align:0.65em}H2O) found at room temperature and pressure as a clear liquid; it is present naturally as rain, and found in rivers, lakes and seas; its solid form is ice and its gaseous form is steam.","The liquid form of this substance: liquid H2O."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To wet or supply with water; to moisten; to overflow with water; to irrigate.\n\n  To pour water into the soil surrounding (plants).\n\n  To provide (animals) with water for drinking.","To pour water into the soil surrounding (plants)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/water"},{"term":"droplet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A very small drop."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/droplet"}],"entries":[{"title":"Drop (liquid)","blocks":[{"text":"A drop or droplet is a small column of liquid, bounded completely or almost completely by free surfaces. A drop may form when liquid accumulates at the end of a tube or other surface boundary, producing a hanging drop called a pendant drop. Drops may also be formed by the condensation of a vapor or by atomization of a larger mass of solid. Water vapor will condense into droplets depending on the temperature. The temperature at which droplets form is called the dew point."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Water_drop_light_art_vijayanrajapuram_01.jpg/330px-Water_drop_light_art_vijayanrajapuram_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drop_(liquid)","wikidata":"Q185789"},{"title":"Water","blocks":[{"text":"Water is an inorganic compound with the chemical formula H2O. It is a transparent, tasteless, odorless, and nearly colorless chemical substance. It is the main constituent of Earth's streams, lakes, and oceans. Water is also the fluid of all known living organisms, in which it acts as a solvent. Water, being a polar molecule, undergoes strong intermolecular hydrogen bonding which is a large contributor to its physical and chemical properties. It is vital for all known forms of life, despite not providing food energy or being an organic micronutrient. Due to its presence in all organisms, its chemical stability, its worldwide abundance, and its strong polarity relative to its small molecular size, water is often referred to as the \"universal solvent\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/H2O_2D_labelled.svg/330px-H2O_2D_labelled.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Water","wikidata":"Q283"},{"title":"Color of water","blocks":[{"text":"The color of water varies with the ambient conditions in which that water is present. While relatively small quantities of water appear to be colorless and transparent, pure water has a slight blue color that becomes deeper as the thickness of the observed sample increases. The hue of water is an intrinsic property and is caused by selective absorption and scattering of blue light. Dissolved elements or suspended impurities may give water a different color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Glass_Half_Full_bw_1.JPG/330px-Glass_Half_Full_bw_1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Color_of_water","wikidata":"Q5148636"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bd/Water_drop_animation_